y extension quantum mechanics it  would also become the backbone of outer  wilds as we know it because working on  these prototypes peaked the team's  curiosity they became interested in  other scientific concepts as well  they dabbled in astrophysics they  explored the idea of nonlinear languages  they envisioned spaceships black holes  and geological anomalies the result was  a mash-up of minigames with no unifying  threat and that's when it hit them this  is no man sky was even announced and so  space exploration games sort of a dead  Shondra let's make a space exploration  game and let's make it kind of make it  feel more like an Apollo 13 or a 2001  then other space exploration games that  had already been made and so from the  beginning the idea was to make something  that was a very much about exploration  an exploration for the sake of curiosity  after graduating from USC Beechum joined  one of his former classmates at Mobius  digital with several group projects  already underway outer wilds took  somewhat of a backseat Beechum and his  colleagues had to work on it in their  spare time slowly fleshing out the idea  that began at USC this concept footage  shows outer wilds in one of those  earlier states as fate would have it  though years of free time led to a  pretty solid foundation in fact by 2015  mobius had an unfinished build of outer  wilds and with the independent Games  Festival looming they decided on a whim  to submit the game for consideration as  it happens it won both the excellence in  design category as well as the Seamus  McNally grand prize despite the team's  other ongoing projects they're showing  at IGF made it clear they really needed  to finish outer wilds and to do so they  would need more like-minded developers  specifically to refine the game's art  direction first this weird like thank  you looking student game that's winning  all these awards was kind of strange  and then like a couple weeks later I  happen to be looking for a job in LA or  yeah I heard about this position here  I guess I like didn't live the prototype  online and like give it a shot and then  it was 4:00 in the morning and I was  like trying to figure out you know how  to get it sooner that dark print ball  and at that point I was like cooked  already and it just sort of naturally  meshed with Who I am as a person I grew  up like in the coastal redwoods living  at my camp I love exploring nature and a  sci-fi concepts and space travel and all  of that and so like as soon as I  actually played the game and so it was  like I basically as a 3d artist Martin  brought a valuable perspective to the  studio in its earlier phases outer wilds  had a major problem  it was promising in a design sense but  it needed personality it was all heady  concepts and intellectual stimuli there  was very little for most players to  relate to that's where Martin came in  along with a newly formed art team he  helped to flesh out the games world with  conifer forests sweeping vistas and  natural rock formations this game about  space travel and quantum physics finally  had a human touch it became a game in  which you could not only watch the Sun  explode you could revel in the beauty of  it so with an emotional element attached  to their prototype mobius had a clearer  vision for the final product after a  successful crowdfunding campaign and a  partnership with Annapurna interactive  all that remained was just to finish the  game but of course game development is  no simple task and outer wilds is no  simple game not only does it play with  space travel in quantum mechanics but  also a concept that video games have  struggled with for decades time itself  partly just because like game worlds  tend to be super static and they don't  do a lot of really you know kind of  crazy interesting things and it's just  seemed like a fun place to explore and  you know you make an exploration game  the world is changing when becomes just  as important as where you need to play  you did get to explore it's time as well  as space and that seemed to mesh really  well and then  because we wanted to make a space  exploration game that was sort of  focused on the sense of real world space  exploration we wanted to make sure that  didn't feel like the world was revolving  around the player and so it was always  important that sort of where the  Majora's Max thing came in because  Majora's Mask does really awesome job  like the only Zelda gamer you don't feel  like everything is just like waiting for  you to do something and that was always  a really important aspect we didn't want  it to feel sort of like the world was  like the world could easily go on and so  originally there was actually a question  it was like okay is this gonna be more  like a roguelike where you knew run Zeus  and this format of the game was gonna be  like you're gonna prepare for each year  each adventure in space you're gonna  kind of like figure out like what type  of probe are you gonna bring like what  type of jetpack and it was gonna be like  okay you like assemble your gear and  then you have you go off into space at  some point you die good job like that's  your run that reset and there were gonna  be more proceed to the rule was gonna be  more procedural it's gonna be more of a  roguelike and the more we thought about  it and kind of worked on it it was just  sort of became clear that that wasn't  really the thing one to spend our time  on drawing from the Afro mentioned Zelda  title as well as comedy classic  Groundhog's Day Mobius settled on a more  elegant structure it's based on  20-minute loops after which the game  worlds Sun explodes engulfing you and  the entire solar system  you're then treated to a rapid replay of  your most recent adventure and then you  wake up and you do it all over again  of course each mini adventure is  different despite the solar system  resetting physically you and in turn the  playable character remember the secrets  you've earned instead of combat perks or  weapon upgrades persisting across  playthroughs outer wilds most valuable  asset is knowledge if most open-world  games are static  how would you in a nutshell describe the  world of outer Wow's never do that by  the way yeah we always joke that outer  worlds is the game you shouldn't try to  make just from like a from a building  the game perspective like we had we used  unity for our engine but like so much of  this stuff in unity it's designed to use  with static objects because that's just  like how you optimize things  in game engines and that's why every  game is like baked lighting all these  things and just from the ground level we  were like we're just not going to have  any static objects in the entire game  and that made it difficult to make heart  it made it difficult to be performant it  made it difficult to do lighting and fog  and like all these things that you kind  of take for her in it as like a game  artist what yeah you can just do this in  a game we just couldn't do those things  but it was like pulling teeth the entire  time to just do even just like simple  level design we were building on spheres  I think programs like Maya are built on  grid systems like you can't just model  things around the sphere easily and so  like I like sort of what your custom  modeling tools of my own so that we  could like extrude things and move them  around spherically yeah every step of  the way it was like okay how are we  going to do this normal game development  thing that everybody does but we can't  because everything's moving and changing  when outer wilds began development space  exploration games were a genre in exile  but as the years continued they  flourished we saw the release of Kerbal  space program elite dangerous and the  infamous juggernaut no man's sky by all  means it was a genre Renaissance and in  the grand scheme of things outer wilds  should have missed out but despite its  origins as a student project despite its  seven-year development cycle  despite the battles its creators had to  fight with the very tools they needed to  make it outer wilds released to  widespread critical acclaim it's now  playable on ps4 PC and Xbox one it even  earned a spot in the latter's coveted  game past program surprise definitely  cuz like at least when I joined the team  like I already played the Alpha built so  I was like I know there's magic here we  just need to not mess it up right and  the whole four years we were worried  about like messing it up we're like  we're gonna ruin it like there's no way  we can actually pull off this crazy  experimental game to the whole quality  that it needs to be at everybody who  like joins the team believe did that  version of the game and so it's really  inspiring and exciting to see people  playing the game  so believe in it the same way that we  did what we were making it people who  are like self-professed Call of Duty  fans and like very little else would  like come out of playtesting like I want  to go learn where about quantum  mechanics or just like do we pay you say  that I don't think we did you know he  was definitely yet towards the end we  were like okay no this is going to work  for at least a lot of people you never  bear to hope that it's gonna you know  have the kind of response that it's that  it's had

